\begin_layout Section*
3.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consumer Theory II: Utility
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In Section 2.2, we proved that if 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is finite, then for each preference relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 admits a utility representation.
 In the consumer's world, 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is not finite (or even countable).
 Thus, this result falls short in this context.
 Are there preferences in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 that do not admit a representation? The following example settles this
 issue.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and define 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{lex}$
\end_inset

 as follows: for each 
\begin_inset Formula $x\equiv(x_{1},x_{2})\in\mathbb{R}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $y\equiv(y_{1},y_{2})\in\mathbb{R}^{2}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim_{lex}y$
\end_inset

 if and only if either 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}>y_{1}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[x_{1}=y_{1}\ \wedge\ x_{2}\geq y_{2}\right]$
\end_inset

.
 One can prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{lex}$
\end_inset

 is a preference relation that does not admit a utility representation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
3.3.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Existence of continuous representation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

There are very general conditions under which we can guarantee a continuous
 preference admits a continuous representation.
 Loosely speaking they are topological properties of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 We will prove 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

simple
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 representation theorem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\emph on
(8).
 
\series default
\emph default
Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $X\equiv\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is a monotone and continuous preference relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 admits a continuous utility representation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 We prove that there is 
\begin_inset Formula $a\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $(a,\ldots,a)\sim x$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $A\equiv\{a\in\mathbb{R}_{+}:(a,\ldots,a)\succsim x\}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B\equiv\{b\in\mathbb{R}_{+}:x\succsim(b,\ldots,b)\}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $A\neq\emptyset$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $a\equiv{\displaystyle \max_{l=1,\ldots,L}}x_{l}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is monotone, then 
\begin_inset Formula $(a+1,\ldots,a+1)\gg x$
\end_inset

 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $(a+1,\ldots,a+1)\succsim x$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $a+1\in A$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $B\ne\emptyset$
\end_inset

.
 We prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $0\in B$
\end_inset

.
 For each 
\begin_inset Formula $n\in\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset

, let 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{n}=\left(x_{1}+\frac{1}{n},\ldots,x_{L}+\frac{1}{n}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 As 
\begin_inset Formula $n\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{n}\rightarrow x$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $n\in\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{n}\gg0$
\end_inset

, then by monotonicity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{n}\succ0$
\end_inset

.
 By continuity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}}x_{n}=x\succsim0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $A\cup B=\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 Clearly, 
\begin_inset Formula $A\cup B\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $a\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is complete, then either 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim(a,\ldots,a)$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $(a,\ldots,a)\succsim x$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, either 
\begin_inset Formula $a\in A$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $a\in B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 are closed.
 We prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is closed.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\{a_{n}\}_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$
\end_inset

 be a sequence in 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 such that as 
\begin_inset Formula $n\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{n}\rightarrow a\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $n\in\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{n}\in A$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $(a_{n},\ldots,a_{n})\succsim x$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, by continuity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}}a_{n}=a\succsim x$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $a\in A$
\end_inset

.
 By a similar arguement, it can be shown that 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is also closed.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is mathematical fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 is connected.
 Hence, since 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 are non-empty closed sets, and 
\begin_inset Formula $A\cup B=\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $A\cap B\ne\emptyset$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $a\in A\cap B$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $(a,\ldots,a)\succsim x\succsim(a,\ldots,a)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $(a,\ldots,a)\sim x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We claim that for each 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

, there is a unique 
\begin_inset Formula $a\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $(a,\ldots,a)\sim x$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\{a,a^{\prime}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 be such that 
\begin_inset Formula $(a,\ldots,a)\sim x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(a^{\prime},\ldots,a^{\prime})\sim x$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose by means of contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $a>a^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 By monotonicity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $(a,\ldots,a)>(a^{\prime},\ldots,a^{\prime})\gg(a,\ldots,a)\succ(a^{\prime},\ldots,a^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 By transitivity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\sim(a,\ldots,a)\succ(a^{\prime},\ldots,a^{\prime})\implies x\succ x$
\end_inset

.
 This contradicts the completeness of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $a\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 is unique.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 be the function defined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{cccl}
u: & \mathbb{R}_{+}^{L} & \rightarrow & \mathbb{R}_{+}\\
 & x & \mapsto & u(x):(u(x),\ldots,u(x))\sim x
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset

We claim that 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,y\}\subseteq X$
\end_inset

.
 We prove will prove 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim y\iff u(x)\geq u(y)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Rightarrow)$
\end_inset

 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)\geq u(y)$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose for contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $u(y)>u(x)$
\end_inset

.
 By the definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y\sim(u(y),\ldots,u(y))>(u(x),\ldots,u(x))\sim x$
\end_inset

.
 So, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $y\gg x$
\end_inset

.
 Then, by monotonicity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y\gg x\implies y\succ x$
\end_inset

.
 However, this contradicts 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)\geq u(y)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(\Leftarrow)$
\end_inset

 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)\geq u(y)$
\end_inset

.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
 By the definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\sim(u(x),\ldots,u(x))\geq(u(y),\ldots,u(y))\sim y$
\end_inset

.
 This implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\geq y$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is monotone and continuous, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is weakly monotone.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\geq y\implies x\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now argue that 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)$
\end_inset

 is a continuous function at all 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

; that is, for any sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x^{t}\}_{t=1}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $x=\lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}x^{t}$
\end_inset

, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}u(x^{t})=u(x)$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, consider a sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x^{t}\}_{t=1}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x=\lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}x^{t}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Note first that the sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\{u(x^{t})\}_{t=1}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 must have a convergent subsequence.
 By monotonicity, for any 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon>0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 lies in a compact subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $[\alpha_{0},\alpha_{1}]$
\end_inset

, for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\Vert x^{\prime}-x\right\Vert \leq\epsilon$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x^{t}\}_{t=1}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 converges to 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, there exists an 
\begin_inset Formula $N\in\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x^{t})$
\end_inset

 lies in this compact set for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t>N$
\end_inset

.
 But any infitine sequence that lies in a compact set must have a convergent
 subsequence.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

What remains is to establish that all convergent subsequences of 
\begin_inset Formula $\{u(x^{t})\}_{t=1}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 converge to 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)$
\end_inset

.
 To see this, suppose otherwise: that there is some strictly increasing
 function 
\begin_inset Formula $m(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 that assigns to each positive integer 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 a positive integer 
\begin_inset Formula $m(t)$
\end_inset

 and for which the subsequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\{u(x^{m(t)})\}_{t=1}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 converges to 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha^{\prime}\neq u(x)$
\end_inset

.
 We first show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha^{\prime}>u(x)$
\end_inset

 leads to a contradiction.
 To being, note that monotonicity would then imply that 
\begin_inset Formula $(\alpha^{\prime},\ldots,\alpha^{\prime})\succ(u(x),\ldots,u(x))$
\end_inset

.
 Now, let 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\alpha}=\frac{1}{2}[\alpha^{\prime}+u(x)]$
\end_inset

.
 The point 
\begin_inset Formula $(\hat{\alpha},\ldots,\hat{\alpha})$
\end_inset

 is the midpoint on 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 between 
\begin_inset Formula $(\alpha^{\prime},\ldots,\alpha^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(u(x),\ldots,u(x))$
\end_inset

.
 By monotonicity, 
\begin_inset Formula $(\hat{\alpha},\ldots,\hat{\alpha})\succ(u(x),\ldots,u(x))$
\end_inset

.
 Now, since 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x^{m(t)})\rightarrow\alpha^{\prime}>\hat{\alpha}$
\end_inset

, there exists an 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{N}$
\end_inset

 such that for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t>\bar{N}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x^{m(t)})>\hat{\alpha}$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, for all such 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{m(t)}\sim(u(x^{m(t)}),\ldots,u(x^{m(t)}))\succ(\hat{\alpha},\ldots,\hat{\alpha})$
\end_inset

 (where the latter relation follows from monotonicity).
 Because preferences are continuous, this would imply that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim(\hat{\alpha},\ldots,\hat{\alpha})$
\end_inset

.
 But since 
\begin_inset Formula $x\sim(u(x),\ldots,u(x))$
\end_inset

, we get 
\begin_inset Formula $(u(x),\ldots,u(x))\succsim(\hat{\alpha},\ldots,\hat{\alpha})$
\end_inset

, which is a contradiciton.
 The argument ruling out 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha^{\prime}<u(x)$
\end_inset

 is similar.
 Thus, since all convergent subsequences of 
\begin_inset Formula $\{u(x^{t})\}_{t=1}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

 must converge to 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)$
\end_inset

, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}u(x^{t})=u(x)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The following theorem states more general conditions for the existence of
 a continuous representation of a preference.
 Indeed, it is enough to assume separability of the alternatives space 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 Since Euclidean spaces are separable, then a continuous preference in a
 consumption space is always representable by a continuous function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\emph on
(9).
 
\series default
\emph default
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a preference on 
\begin_inset Formula $X\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is continuous, then there is a continuous function 
\begin_inset Formula $u:X\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 representing 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
The proof can be found in (Debreu, 1963).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark*
From Theorem 9 and Proposition 7: Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a preference relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $X\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is continuous on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists$
\end_inset

 a continuous utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $u:X\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 representing 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, the continuity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is not only necessary for the existence of a continuous utility function
 representation, but it is also sufficient.
 This necessary and sufficient condition holds when the consumption space
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $X\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 However, when the set of alternatives is in a topological space other than
 a Euclidean space, a continuous preference may not be represented by a
 continuous function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As notes in Section 2.2.2, the utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 that represents a preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is not unique; any strictly increasing transformation of 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

, say 
\begin_inset Formula $v(x)=f(u(x))$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is a strictly increasing function, also represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

.
 Theorem 9 tells us that if 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is continuous, there exists 
\emph on
some
\emph default
 continuous utility function representing 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

.
 But not all utility functions representing 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 are continuous.
 In fact, a continuous preference can be represented by a discontinuous
 utility function; any strictly increasing but discontinuous transformation
 of a continuous utility function also represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

.
 However, a discontinuous preference cannot be represented by a continuous
 utility function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
If preferences are monotone, then the utility function is strictly increasing:
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $x\gg y$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)>u(y)$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ u$
\end_inset

 representing 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
3.3.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Further requirements on preferences
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Convex Preferences
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Restrictions on preferences translate into restrictions on the form of utility
 functions.
 The property of monotonicity, for example, implies that the utility function
 is strictly increasing: if 
\begin_inset Formula $x\gg y$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)>u(y)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The property of convexity of preferences, on the other hand, implies that
 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is 
\emph on
quasiconcave
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(56).

\series default
 
\emph on
A function 
\begin_inset Formula $u:\mathbb{R}^{L}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 is 
\series bold
quasi-concave 
\series default
iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{x,y\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{L},\ \forall\ \alpha\in[0,1]:u(\alpha x+(1-\alpha)y)\geq\min\{u(x),u(y)\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Similarly, strict convexity of preferences implies strict concavity of 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(57).

\series default
 
\emph on
A function 
\begin_inset Formula $u:\mathbb{R}^{L}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 is 
\series bold
