 utility function that has the specified form.
 Increasingness and quasiconcavity are 
\emph on
ordinal
\emph default
 properties of 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

; they are preserved for any arbitrary increasing transformation of the
 utility index.
 In contrast, the special forms of the utility representations in Theorem
 10 and Proposition 9 are not preserved; they are 
\emph on
cardinal
\emph default
 properties that are simply convenient choice for a utility representation.
 Thus, in this sense, continuity is also a cardinal property of utility
 functions.
\end_layout

