\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 be a Cobb-Douglas preference on 
\begin_inset Formula $X\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 By definition, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is represented by a utility funciton 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 of the form 
\begin_inset Formula $x\equiv(x_{1},x_{2})\in X\mapsto u(x)\equiv x_{1}^{\alpha}x_{2}^{1-\alpha}$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

First, we prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is homothetic.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,y\}\subseteq X:x\sim y$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda x\sim\lambda y$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x\sim y\iff x_{1}^{\alpha}x_{2}^{1-\alpha}=y_{1}^{\alpha}y_{2}^{1-\alpha}$
\end_inset

.
 Multiplying both sides by 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda x_{1}^{\alpha}x_{2}^{1-\alpha}=\lambda y_{1}^{\alpha}y_{2}^{1-\alpha}\iff(\lambda x_{1})^{\alpha}(\lambda x_{2})^{1-\alpha}=(\lambda y_{1})^{\alpha}(\lambda y_{2})^{1-\alpha}$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\lambda x_{1},\lambda x_{2})=u(\lambda y_{1},\lambda y_{2})$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $u(\lambda x_{1},\lambda x_{2})=u(\lambda y_{1},\lambda y_{2})\iff\lambda x\sim\lambda y$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is homothetic.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Next, we prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is monotone.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,y\}\subseteq X:x\gg y$
\end_inset

.
 We want to show 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succ y$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $x\gg y$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}^{\alpha}x_{2}^{1-\alpha}>y_{1}^{\alpha}y_{2}^{1-\alpha}$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x_{1},x_{2})>u(y_{1},y_{2})$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x_{1},x_{2})>u(y_{1},y_{2})\iff x\succ y$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is monotone.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Finally, we show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is not strictly monotone.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,y\}\subseteq X:(x_{1},0)\gneq(y_{1},0)$
\end_inset

.
 Now, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}^{\alpha}(0)^{1-\alpha}=0=y_{1}^{\alpha}(0)^{1-\alpha}$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x_{1},0)=u(y_{1},0)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x_{1},0)=u(y_{1},0)\iff(x_{1},0)\sim(y_{1},0)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $(x_{1},0)\nsucc(y_{1},0)$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is not strictly monotone.
\end_layout

