\begin_layout Proof
Consider an 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

- commmodity space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and a preference 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 A preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is homothetic if its indifference sets are related by proportional expansion
 along rays, i.e., for each pair 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,y\}\subseteq X$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\sim y$
\end_inset

 and each 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha x\sim\alpha y$
\end_inset

.
 A preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 is continuous if it is a closed subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x^{t}\underset{t\to\infty}{\longrightarrow}x,\ \forall\ y^{t}\underset{t\to\infty}{\longrightarrow}y:\left(\forall t\in\mathbb{N}:x^{t}\succsim y^{t}\right)\implies x\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
 We want to prove that if preferences are homothetic and continuous, then
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{x,y\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L},\ \forall\ \alpha>0:x\succsim y\implies\alpha x\succsim\alpha y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

(Method 1): Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,y\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}:x\succsim y$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha x\succsim\alpha y$
\end_inset

.
 By Theorem 10, a continuous preference 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is homothetic if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 admits a continuous representation 
\begin_inset Formula $u:X\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 such that for each 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\alpha x)=\alpha u(x)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim y\iff u(x)\geq u(y)$
\end_inset

.
 Multiplying both sides by 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha u(x)\geq\alpha u(y)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is homogenous of degree one, then 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\alpha x)=\alpha u(x)\geq\alpha u(y)=u(\alpha y)$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\alpha x)\geq u(\alpha y)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\alpha x)\geq u(\alpha y)\iff\alpha x\succsim\alpha y$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, if preferences 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 are homoethetic and continuous, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{x,y\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L},\ \forall\ \alpha>0:x\succsim y\implies\alpha x\succsim\alpha y$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

(Method 2): Assume preferences are homothetic and continuous.
 Suppose for contradiction 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ \{x,y\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+}:x\succsim y\ \wedge\ \alpha y\succ\alpha x$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is continuous, then by Theorem 9, there exists a continuous function 
\begin_inset Formula $u:X\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 representing 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim y\iff u(x)\geq u(y)$
\end_inset

.
 Also, 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha y\succ\alpha x\iff u(\alpha y)>u(\alpha x)$
\end_inset

.
 Define 
\begin_inset Formula $f(t)=u(tx)-u(ty)$
\end_inset

.
 Note, 
\begin_inset Formula $f(1)\geq0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\alpha)<0$
\end_inset

.
 So, by the Intermediate Value Theorem, 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ \alpha^{*}\in(\alpha,1]\ \vee\ \alpha^{*}\in[1,\alpha)$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\alpha^{*})=0$
\end_inset

.
 This means that 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\alpha^{*}x)=u(\alpha^{*}y)$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\alpha^{*}x)=u(\alpha^{*}y)\iff\alpha^{*}x\sim\alpha^{*}y$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 is homoethetic, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\nicefrac{\alpha}{\alpha^{*}}\right)\alpha^{*}x\sim\left(\nicefrac{\alpha}{\alpha^{*}}\right)\alpha^{*}y$
\end_inset

, and thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha x\sim\alpha y$
\end_inset

.
 But this contradicts 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha y\succ\alpha x$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $(\rightarrow\leftarrow)$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, if preferences 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim$
\end_inset

 are homoethetic and continuous, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{x,y\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L},\ \forall\ \alpha>0:x\succsim y\implies\alpha x\succsim\alpha y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Let 
